&lt;p&gt;This month&amp;rsquo;s seminar hosted by the Center for Sexual and Gender Minority Health Research at Columbia University School of Nursing will feature LaRon E. Nelson, PhD, RN, FNP, FNAP, FNYAM, FAAN, Independence Foundation Professor of Nursing at Yale University School of Nursing. The title of his talk is &amp;ldquo;Leveraging Commercial Determinants to Advance Sexual and Gender Minority Health.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Nelson&amp;rsquo;s research focuses on implementation science and multi-level interventions to reduce race- and sexuality-based disparities in HIV outcomes, with particular attention to Black men who have sex with men and African, Caribbean, and Black communities. His presentation will explore how commercial determinants can be leveraged to advance sexual and gender minority health and inform more equitable approaches to research, practice, and public health intervention.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is open to everyone!&amp;nbsp;Attendees may join virtually or in person; however, we ask that those planning to attend in person RSVP in advance for logistical purposes. Virtually attendees can join via:&lt;a href="http://&nbsp;https://columbiacuimc.zoom.us/j/96350461208?pwd=1atmR8XuIBAUKV4yUADdk5H9hFIT5X.1." target="_blank"&gt;&amp;nbsp;https://columbiacuimc.zoom.us/j/96350461208?pwd=1atmR8XuIBAUKV4yUADdk5H9hFIT5X.1&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this El Ni&amp;ntilde;o year, anticipatory action projects are taking action using forecasts right now, as droughts and floods are happening around the globe, they are activating millions of dollars of pre-funded disaster action before the disasters happen.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are tough tradeoffs for disaster managers between acting earlier and having enough accuracy. The people responsible for managing disasters must have true agency and governance as they decide how much to bank on forecasts to carefully bridge the gap between the science and decisionmaking. It is important to work hard to improve transparency to have something more than a renamed alert system that truly honors sovereignty for climate justice.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this highly interactive session, participants will use actual project tools, inviting perspectives from high level leadership to nitty gritty implementers and explore how to move forward with the challenges in Anticipatory Action. Critically, we will work through ways that communities and local leadership can drive its design and operation.&lt;/p&gt;
