&lt;p&gt;Please join the Program on U.S.-Russia Relations at the Harriman Institute for a book talk by &lt;strong&gt;Thomas Kent&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by &lt;strong&gt;Elise Giuliano&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Russia-Ukraine war has been fought as fiercely in the information sphere as in on-the-ground combat. Vladimir Putin&amp;rsquo;s propaganda machine has worked to sustain Russian domestic support for his invasion despite staggering military losses. Ukraine has used persuasion tactics to strengthen its people&amp;rsquo;s resolve and persuade Western democracies to continue sending arms and money. Western nations have tried to build support for Ukraine in the Global South. &lt;em&gt;The Propaganda Manual of the Ukraine War&lt;/em&gt; describes overt and covert persuasion tactics by governments, intelligence services, media organizations, and private actors on all sides of the war, such as forged websites, hacked broadcasts, attempts to manipulate social networks and AI, and arson and bombing of civilian targets in West Europe and Russia. The book describes which tactics have had the most effect and makes recommendations for Western information operations in future conflicts. It also assesses Western media coverage of the war, including a tendency to exaggerate both victories and defeats and an insufficient focus on events inside Russia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for a discussion on the dangers caused by climate change - particularly sea level rise, which is certain to cause future displacement. The specific obligations of states toward displaced populations, and the thresholds for triggering such obligations, remain nascent and contested. Though no dedicated multilateral treaty addresses the rights of people displaced by climate change, recent interpretive guidance from the Human Rights Committee and the International Court of Justice affirms that such state obligations exist and are rooted in existing international law.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Those seeking to prove a violation of the right to life - as a basis for claims to extraterritorial protection amid climate change - face significant obstacles, including the lack of a standardized norm for investigating and documenting climate-related ill treatment and imminent harm from a medico-legal perspective. This panel explores whether a new medico-legal protocol for climate displacement evidence is needed, and how such a protocol could support both duty bearers and rights holders in understanding the nature of states&amp;#39; legal obligations.&lt;/p&gt;
