&lt;p&gt;Book Talk:&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Enduring Hostility: The Making of America&amp;rsquo;s Iran Policy&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A discussion of Dalia Dassa Kayes&amp;rsquo; book and the ongoing war with Iran&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wednesday, Sept 23, 2026&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3pm-4pm&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1302 IAB&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With &lt;strong&gt;Dalia Dassa Kaye&lt;/strong&gt;, Senior Fellow, Burkle Center for International Relations, University of California, Los Angeles&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Moderated by &lt;strong&gt;Stacie Goddard&lt;/strong&gt;, Director, Saltzman Institute of War and Peace Studies&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Book Description:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;US&amp;ndash;Iran hostility has endured for almost as long as the Cold War. Momentous geopolitical shifts, changing leaderships, and evolving domestic priorities have not fundamentally altered this antagonistic relationship. Standard explanations pin the blame for this enduring hostility on Iran and its leaders&amp;#39; revolutionary ideology and policies at odds with the United States and the West. While Iran bears significant blame for a deeply adversarial relationship&amp;mdash;the country often engages in dangerous and repressive activities&amp;mdash;this book demonstrates that &amp;quot;it&amp;#39;s them, not us&amp;quot; accounts cannot alone explain America&amp;#39;s posture toward this complicated but critically important country.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drawing on original interviews with former government officials, oral histories, memoirs, congressional hearings, archival material, and the author&amp;#39;s own participation in dozens of Iran-related track two meetings, Dalia Dassa Kaye deftly explores how America&amp;#39;s Iran policy is made, the people who make it, and the underlying ideas and perceptions that inform it. Dassa Kaye looks back at US policy toward Iran over the past four decades to help us look ahead, offering wider lessons for understanding American foreign policymaking and providing critical insights at a pivotal time of heightened military tensions in and around the Middle East.&lt;em&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note on campus access:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Access to Columbia&amp;#39;s campus is currently limited to current students, faculty, and staff. Alumni can register for same day access using&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.alumni.columbia.edu/content/alumni-access-morningside-campus" target="_blank"&gt;this link&lt;/a&gt;. If you have any questions concerns about campus or event accessibility, please email Saltzman Institute Coordinator OG Grinberg at&amp;nbsp;ojg2112@columbia.edu.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A conversation with a&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Special Representative of the Ukrainian Government&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;and&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/valentina-izmirlieva/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Valentina Izmirlieva&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Professor of Slavic Languages and Literatures; Director of the Harriman Institute&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Ukraine is often described through the lens of resilience &amp;mdash; the remarkable ability of its people to withstand war, uncertainty, and profound disruption. But resilience alone does not tell the full story of Ukraine. To understand the country today &amp;mdash; its choices, its strength, and its vision for the future &amp;mdash; we must also understand its history, culture, identity, and place in the world.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this World Leaders Forum conversation, a special representative will explore why deeper knowledge of Ukraine matters far beyond its borders. Drawing on Ukraine&amp;rsquo;s experience of resilience and on their work to advance Ukrainian studies globally, they will reflect on the role universities can play in turning global attention to Ukraine into lasting knowledge and understanding.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hosted at Columbia University, home to a longstanding tradition of Ukrainian studies at the Harriman Institute, the conversation will invite students to consider a broader question: What can understanding Ukraine teach us about the world we live in today &amp;mdash; and the future we are shaping together?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Columbia University students are invited to participate in a moderated question-and-answer session.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Advance&amp;nbsp;registration is required for in-person attendance.&lt;/p&gt;
