&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;American Body Politics: Healthy Bodies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.ircpl.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Institute for Religion, Culture and Public Life&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;for an event in our&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;American Body Politics &lt;/strong&gt;series.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Date: &lt;/strong&gt;Thursday, September 24, 2026 from 12:30-2:00PM&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Location:&lt;/strong&gt; Calder Lounge (107 Uris Hall)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers:&lt;/strong&gt; Anthony Petro (University of Notre Dame) and Hannah Zaves-Greene (Brandeis University)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Moderator:&lt;/strong&gt; Jamie Marsella (University of Toronto)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;What does it mean to be healthy? Are we responsible for the health of others? Are we responsible for our own? The concept of health in the United States is slippery, built on an ever-shifting foundation of scientific, religious, and moral ideas. As the Covid-19 pandemic made painfully clear, moments of health crisis surface competing visions of health, revealing the ways our existing systems of care ignore the needs of the disabled, people of color, and the LGBTQ community. This conversation brings together historians of religion, science, and sexuality in the twentieth century to situate our current moment in a longer history of health and disability in the United States as both a scientific and religious question.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://forms.gle/KVeC5uf7wpQobPrV8" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Register here&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;IIJS Prof. &lt;strong&gt;Rebecca Kobrin, &lt;/strong&gt;in conversation with &lt;strong&gt;Prof. Kim Phillips-Fein&lt;/strong&gt;, joins to discuss her recent book &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Credit to the Nation&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and how entrepreneurial Jewish immigrants transformed commercial banking and enabled the economic and social advancement of Jews in America.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thursday, September 24, at 1:00 PM | 617 Kent Hall&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Credit to the Nation&lt;/em&gt; traces the novel practices of bankers who not only enabled the flourishing of American Jewry but also revolutionized the US financial industry. Drawing on previously unexamined archival materials in Russian, Yiddish, German, and English, Kobrin tells a story that is also crucial to the history of New York, as immigrant bankers&amp;rsquo; financing of real estate transformed wide swathes of the city. Lenders drove a boom in the prices of tenement buildings, but heavy speculation eventually precipitated the downfall of immigrant banking. Kobrin notes in particular the case of the Bank of United States&amp;mdash;a private lender catering primarily to Jewish businessmen&amp;mdash;which the Federal Reserve refused to bail out from bankruptcy in 1930. Immigrants&amp;rsquo; grasping for credit, and the rise and fall of immigrant banks, gave way to a contemporary banking industry that, ironically, refuses credit to today&amp;rsquo;s immigrants. Kobrin reminds us that now, as before, the denial of credit pushes entrepreneurial Americans into unregulated money-lending and the trap of endless debt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rebecca Kobrin&lt;/strong&gt; is the Russell and Bettina Knapp Associate Professor of American Jewish History, in Columbia University&amp;rsquo;s Department of History, where she teaches in the field of American Jewish History, specializing in modern Jewish migration. She also served as the Co-Director of the Institute for Israel and Jewish Studies at Columbia.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kim Phillips-Fein&lt;/strong&gt; is a historian of 20th-century American politics and political economy, whose interests include the history of political institutions and ideas, the history of labor and capitalism and the history of New York City.&lt;em&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;She comes to Columbia from New York University, where she taught at the Gallatin School of Individualized Study and the History Department of the College of Arts and Sciences.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
