&lt;p&gt;How does a musical tradition survive over 800 years, when nothing is written down? The vocal tradition of &lt;em&gt;khayal&lt;/em&gt; (imagination) has been sustained by eight centuries of oral transmission, with the Saami family as its torchbearers in the 21st century. Without notation, a convention common to Western classical music, &lt;em&gt;khayal&lt;/em&gt; has been passed down entirely through oral and aural practice. Across generations, this tradition has relied on master-student relationships built on trust and a consistent practice of refining the ear, voice, and heart, allowing khayal to persist into our present moment.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Saami brothers, guided by Naseeruddin Saami, the last living master of the 49-note microtonal vocal scale (&lt;em&gt;shrutis&lt;/em&gt;), belong to a musical past that can be traced back to 13th-century Delhi, filled with echoes of melodies emanating from the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya&amp;rsquo;s shrine. Their ancestor was Miyan Samit bin Ibrahim, the one and only disciple of Amir Khusro. Together, they blended Persian melodic modes, Arabic &lt;em&gt;maqaam&lt;/em&gt;, Turkish musical elements, and South Asian traditions to create &lt;em&gt;khayal&lt;/em&gt;, and its devotional offshoot, &lt;em&gt;qawwali&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The concert will be preceded by a 5pm conversation with Ustad Rauf Saami, and Nermeen Arastu (Professor of Law, student of Ustad Rauf, and Co-Lead, Strategy and Residencies for the Saami family) where we unpack the history of &lt;em&gt;khayal&lt;/em&gt;, the importance of oral transmission, what the future holds for living traditions that resist full inscription, and how scholarship and practice might sustain one another in that effort. The Saami family &lt;em&gt;khayal&lt;/em&gt; performance will begin at 6pm.&lt;/p&gt;
