&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;Connecting Proteomics with Neuroimaging to Decode Brain Aging and Dementia&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keenan Walker, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Senior Investigator&lt;br /&gt;
National Institute on Aging (NIA), Intramural Research Program (IRP)&lt;br /&gt;
National Institutes of Health&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plasma proteomics provides a scalable window into systemic biology, while neuroimaging captures structural, vascular, and neurodegenerative changes in the brain. In this talk, I will describe how large-scale plasma proteomic profiling can be integrated with longitudinal neuroimaging, cognition, genetics, and cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers to identify proteins and pathways associated with brain aging and dementia risk. Following initially cognitively normal individuals over time can reveal molecular signals that precede imaging changes and cognitive impairment, distinguish early disease processes from downstream consequences, and clarify heterogeneity in progression. These approaches may improve biologically informed risk prediction and identify new targets for preventing and treating age-related brain disease.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This research seminar is hosted by the Center for Innovation in Imaging Biomarkers and Integrated Diagnostics (CIMBID)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
